<title>LUXEMBOURG: Bundesbank official warns against wide EMU.</title>
<headline>Bundesbank official warns against wide EMU.</headline>
<dateline>LUXEMBOURG 1996-10-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>Bundesbank Council member Hans Juergen Koebenick has warned against creating a single currency that would run the risk of collapse by including too many countries.</p>
<p>In an interview to be broadcast in BBC's nine o'clock news on Monday, Koebenick suggested that countries such as Italy and Spain might be excluded on the grounds that they would weaken the credibility of the single currency, the euro.</p>
<p>&quot;It must be clear to everyone that there is no return,&quot; Koebenick said.</p>
<p>&quot;When we start on the false foot, we all fall together,&quot; he added.</p>
<p>While Koebenick has in the past taken a hard line position on economic and monetary union (EMU), his remarks follow those of Bundesbank president Hans Tietmeyer, who last week argued for credible and durable economic convergence for those joining the single currency in 1999.</p>
<p>-- Brussels newroom +32 2 287-6830</p>
